  "description": "Construct the orthogonal projection matrix onto the column space of a full-column-rank matrix.\n\nOrthogonal projectors satisfy idempotency P^2 = P and symmetry P^T = P.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nP = A(A^\\top A)^{-1} A^\\top\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute Gram matrix A^T A\n2. Invert Gram matrix and multiply P = A (A^T A)^(-1) A^T\n\n### Returns\n2D matrix P of shape (m, m).",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Orthogonal Projection Matrix\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Orthogonal Projection Matrix** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nOrthogonal projectors satisfy idempotency P^2 = P and symmetry P^T = P. In this problem, the goal is to: construct the orthogonal projection matrix onto the column space of a full-column-rank matrix.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nP = A(A^\\top A)^{-1} A^\\top\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute Gram matrix A^T A\n2. **Phase 2**: Invert Gram matrix and multiply P = A (A^T A)^(-1) A^T\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: A = [[1.0, 0.0], [1.0, 1.0], [0.0, 1.0]]\n\nOutput: [[0.666667, 0.333333, -0.333333], [0.333333, 0.666667, 0.333333], [-0.333333, 0.333333, 0.666667]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_orthogonal_projection under standard test conditions."
  ],
